{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And they shall make the ephod.</b> In all the other [instructions] it says ״וְעָשִׂיתָ״ ('and you shall make'), but here it says ״וְעָשׂוּ״ ('and they shall make'), because our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Arakhin 16a) that the ephod comes to atone for idolatry, and here Israel transgressed and made the calf — therefore let them all engage in the ephod, so that it may serve as atonement for them all. And in this same manner our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 34:2) concerning ״וְעָשׂוּ אֲרוֹן״ — 'let all come and engage in it,' etc. So too ״וְעָשׂוּ״ said here, for Moses had no share at all in that deed; therefore it was hung upon Israel and not upon Moses. And above we have explained it in another way. And He said that it shall be ״מַעֲשֵׂה חֹשֵׁב״ ('the work of a weaver/thinker'), in order to atone for idolatry, in which the thought is accounted as the deed, as it says (Ezekiel 14:5) ״לְמַעַן תְּפֹשׂ אֶת בֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּלִבָּם״. And the reason for this is that the essence of that sin depends on faith — that is, in thought — and all the acts of service that one performs for idolatry are subordinate to the thought, for the essence of that sin lies in the believing heart. And this is what it says: ״וְחֵשֶׁב אֲפֻדָּתוֹ אֲשֶׁר עָלָיו כְּמַעֲשֵׂהוּ״ — for even though חֵשֶׁב in its plain sense means חֵבֶשׁ, that is, a band that one girds, none the less it employed the term ״חֵשֶׁב״ which alludes to מַחֲשָׁבָה (thought), to expound from it that in it the thought is כְּמַעֲשֵׂהוּ — that is, accounted as the deed. For it was for this reason that the חֵשֶׁב was made כְּמַעֲשֵׂהוּ, to atone for the thought that is accounted as the deed." "And for this reason we find in parashat Shelach, in the passage ״וְכִי תִשְׁגּוּ״ etc. (Numbers 15:22–24), which speaks of idolatry, that there He preceded the olah to the chatat — even though in every other atonement the chatat precedes the olah. For in every sin the reflection preceded the deed, and none the less the deed is weightier than the reflection; therefore He removes the greater one first — and that is the chatat, which atones for the deed — and afterward the olah for the spirit of reflection. But in idolatry the sin of thought is greater than the deed; therefore He precedes the olah to the chatat. And so here too He preceded the atonement for thought to the atonement for deed, for first He said ״מַעֲשֵׂה חֹשֵׁב״ to atone for the sin of thought, and afterward He said ״שְׁתֵּי כְתֵפֹת חֹבְרֹת יִהְיֶה לּוֹ״ to atone for the sin of deed, as it is written (Isaiah 46:7) concerning idolaters: ״יִשָּׂאֻהוּ עַל כָּתֵף״ etc. And because the calf caused the breaking of the tablets that Moses carried upon his two shoulders, and they were five commandments opposite five, and they were joined — for the commandments were parallel, one opposite the other — therefore there came an atonement of ״שְׁתֵּי כְתֵפֹת חֹבְרֹת יִהְיֶה לּוֹ״ etc. And in addition to this atonement, the ark was made, which was carried upon two shoulders by the two staves of the ark, and this is what it says (Deuteronomy 33:12) ״וּבֵין כְּתֵפָיו שָׁכֵן״ — for the Holy One, blessed be He, contracted His Shekhinah between the two staves of the ark that rested upon the shoulders of the ark-bearers. And further, the Holy One, blessed be He, contracted His Shekhinah between the two shoulders of the priest wearing the ephod, for upon it hung the breastplate of judgment, in which were the Urim and Tumim opposite his heart, and the Ineffable Name was engraved upon them."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:59.440569043Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}